# Documentation lifecycle

This rule is how documents are classified, closed, and kept from
regenerating the 15k-line FOLLOWUPS / 26k-line CHANGELOG failure mode.
Cite from [`91-documentation-after-plans.mdc`](91-documentation-after-plans.mdc).
shekyl-core's front door, archive checklist and document gates are core
rule [`195-documentation-lifecycle-core`](https://github.com/Shekyl-Foundation/shekyl-core/blob/dev/.cursor/rules/195-documentation-lifecycle-core.mdc).

## Document classes

| Class | Lives in | Banner |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Contract of record | `docs/` or `docs/design/` forever | `Status: LIVING CONTRACT` + last-verified date |
| Active plan | `docs/design/` | `Status: OPEN` + round |
| Closed plan / preflight / retired concept | `docs/completed/` | `Status: CLOSED-as-record` or `RETIRED` |
| Operator / user | `docs/` root | no round archaeology |
| Ledger | FOLLOWUPS, CHANGELOG, decision log | not a work queue |
| Map | `IMPLEMENTATION_INDEX.md` | identifier + owning doc + one-line status |

The paths are shekyl-core's; a repository with another layout names its
equivalents in its own rules.

Do **not** implement from `docs/completed/`. Git history is the archive.

## Status banner

Every file under `docs/design/` and `docs/completed/` carries a
**Status** line in the first 40 lines after the H1.

**The banner is not the grep surface.** It is the first thing a reader
who *opens the file* sees. It is invisible to the reader who arrives by
`grep`/`rg` at line 1160 — and arriving mid-file is the normal case for
an agent, which reads what the regex matched rather than scanning from
the H1. A banner is necessary and is not sufficient.

**Status must therefore be line-local.** Any assertion a reader could
mistake for current carries its own status *in the grep-visible line*:
the heading, the table row, or the sentence itself — never only in a
banner or an enclosing heading above it. Concretely:

- A **section** whose status differs from its file's says so **in its
  own heading** (`### 5.5 … — RULED 2026-09-04`), because a heading is
  what a section-level grep returns.
- A **row** asserting a state carries its **era or status token in the
  row**: `| CEN-x | CHECKED-CONFORMANT | Reviewed at `<sha>` …`. A pin
  in a table *header* governs rows that grep returns without it.
- A **superseded artifact** — an old table, formula, or sim row — is
  either deleted or carries the marker in each line, not in a
  heading. The artifact is what gets mistaken for current; a line that
  says `REJECTED` or `SUPERSEDED` is hard to mistake.

**What line-local status does not mean: delete the past.** A pinned
*records-was* row — `DIVERGENT at <sha>`, a conformance verdict at a
declared era — is a **provenance-bearing claim that is true now**, not
history, and it stays. The discriminator is **artifact vs verdict**,
not past vs present. A verdict carries its era in-line and is legible
to a grep reader; a superseded artifact carries no self-describing
status and is what must go. Moving content to git history does not make
it safe — `git log -S` is precisely the move a grep-driven reader does
not make.

## Completing a plan includes archive-or-contract

When the work a plan doc owns is done:

- If it is a living contract of record, it **stays** in `docs/design/`
  (or `docs/`) and the banner flips to `LIVING CONTRACT` with a
  last-verified date.
- If it is a plan / preflight / closeout / retired concept, `git mv` it
  to `docs/completed/` in the **same** change, following the repository's
  archive checklist (shekyl-core's: index §8).

“Implementation landed” alone is not archival. Mixed docs that still
own named open residue stay in `design/`.

## FOLLOWUPS is a queue of one-liners

Each item is **≤20 lines**: one sentence of what remains, a `Target:`
from the three tokens below, an **`Owner:`** sub-bullet, and a link to the
owning doc. Essays belong in the plan doc or the completed record, not in
the queue.

`Owner:` names what the row **resolves to**: a live design document. A PR
number may be cited beside it but is not an owner on its own — PRs merge
and close, and a row whose only owner is a merged PR is a row nobody owns.
Six rows once named "the E2 lane" as owner while no such lane existed in
any form; that is the shape the cell makes visible (rule 22).
shekyl-core's gate and its other owner kind are core rule
[`195-documentation-lifecycle-core`](https://github.com/Shekyl-Foundation/shekyl-core/blob/dev/.cursor/rules/195-documentation-lifecycle-core.mdc).

Allowed `Target:` values only:

- **pre-genesis** — default. Lands before genesis if it should exist at
  launch.
- **post-genesis** — exceptional deferral with a **named blocker** why
  it cannot land before genesis. The list stays tiny.
- **V4** — lattice-only transition, 2–5 years, gated on NIST (or
  successor) actually approving primitives such as lattice threshold
  signatures. Not a parking lot.

`V3.1` / `V3.2` / `V3.x` / `V5+` are not work-item targets. `V3.1` may
remain in a historical protocol *title*.

Resolved items are **removed**. Git history is the archive.

How many rows an item gets — zero, exactly one, or none-but-symbols — is the
disposition question, and `23-disposition-visibility.mdc` owns it. This section owns
only what a row looks like once the disposition says there is one.

## CHANGELOG scope

`docs/CHANGELOG.md` records user-visible, consensus, API, or
security-relevant deltas. Design-round archaeology stays in the plan
doc. A plan that only rearranges documentation does not owe a
CHANGELOG novel; a one-line Unreleased note is enough when the change
is process-only.

## Who wins

Code on `dev` > living contract > implementation index (map) >
plan-doc status table > FOLLOWUPS. Fix the loser; do not
drive-by-update every mention.

